      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Absolutely, let's strip away the poetic metaphors and dive into the technical nitty-gritty of the pressure differential and Bernoulli's principle.\n\nWhen air flows over the curved top surface of a wing, it has to travel a longer distance compared to the air flowing underneath the flatter bottom surface. According to Bernoulli's principle, as the velocity of a fluid (in this case, air) increases, its pressure decreases. So, the faster-moving air over the top of the wing results in lower pressure compared to the slower-moving air beneath the wing.\n\nMathematically, Bernoulli's equation can be expressed as P + 0.5ρv^2 + ρgh = constant, where P is the pressure, ρ is the air density, v is the velocity of the air, and h is the height above a reference point. In the context of an airfoil, the height (h) remains relatively constant, so the equation simplifies to P + 0.5ρv^2 = constant. This means that as the velocity (v) of the air increases over the top surface of the wing, the pressure (P) must decrease to keep the equation balanced.\n\nThe pressure differential created by this velocity difference",
